 \subsection{Secondary families and calibration}\label{app:families}
 Table~\ref{tab:jointbrier} applies the same stepdown procedure to the six Brier-score contrasts. Here QQQ and IWM both survive, with adjusted $p$-values of 0.006 and 0.041, and SPY does not (0.067). The Brier family is adjusted within itself: its $p$-values are not comparable with those of Table~\ref{tab:joint}, and the difference between the two score families is a difference in how the scores weight errors, not a contradiction. The claim in the text that only one asset shows a score advantage therefore refers to log loss and not to scores in general.
 
-Table~\ref{tab:jointecon} does the same for six mean net-return contrasts at 5 basis points per side. IWM survives on both contrasts: the unrestricted-minus-IID difference (adjusted $p=0.020$) and the reversible-minus-unrestricted difference (0.047), the latter negative in sign, that is, against the restricted model. SPY and QQQ do not survive (0.171 and 0.531). This family inherits the allocation rule and the cost scenario, and no power claim is made for it.
-
-Table~\ref{tab:calibration} reports a count-weighted calibration error with a block-bootstrap interval. The statistic is small in absolute terms, between 0.026 and 0.040, but only three to seven of the thirty possible bins hold thirty or more observations, so it is dominated by a handful of cells and the intervals are as wide as the estimates themselves. It supports the descriptive reading of Figure~\ref{fig:calibration} and not a claim of calibrated probabilities.
+Table~\ref{tab:jointecon} applies the procedure to one exploratory family of twelve economic contrasts: six mean net-return differences and six Sharpe differences, all at 5 basis points per side. Four Sharpe contrasts survive. SPY and IWM gain on unrestricted minus IID (adjusted $p=0.032$ and $0.001$) and lose on reversible minus unrestricted (0.006 and 0.007, with negative estimates, that is, against the restricted model). Among mean net returns only IWM's unrestricted advantage survives (0.022); its reversible contrast falls just outside (0.054), and the SPY and QQQ net contrasts do not survive (0.184 and 0.555). The family inherits the allocation rule and the cost scenario, and no power claim is made for it.
+
+Table~\ref{tab:calibration} reports the count-weighted calibration error, its block-bootstrap interval and a parametric bootstrap p-value for the null that each day's outcome is drawn from that day's forecast. Every asset and model is rejected ($p\le0.004$). The rejection is driven by the marginal distribution rather than by the shape of the reliability curve: the cut points are estimated from history, and the final period realised class frequencies of 29.9/37.3/32.9 for SPY, 29.1/39.2/31.6 for QQQ and 32.8/36.7/30.6 for IWM, instead of one third each. The test therefore says that the level of the forecasts does not match the realised marginal; it does not identify a conditional calibration failure, and with only three to seven populated bins the individual cells carry little information.
 
